Hello love, here is all the family news since my last letter.

Thank you for the birthday card. It arrived on the day, and the picture on th=
e front made me laugh; it looks just like the cottage we stayed in all those =
years ago.

The choir concert went well. We were nervous about the new piece, but it came=
 together on the night, and the church was nearly full, which was lovely to s=
ee.

We watched the meteor shower from the back garden last night. It was cold, bu=
t we saw several bright ones, and the children talked about it all through br=
eakfast.

The children are back at school and the house is very quiet. I have been catc=
hing up on the jobs I kept putting off over the summer, starting with the cup=
board under the stairs.

Dad has been busy in his workshop building a bird table. It is a little lopsi=
ded, but the robins do not seem to mind, and he watches them from the kitchen=
 every morning.

I took up watercolours again after many years. My first few attempts were mud=
dy, but the evening class has helped, and I am quite pleased with a painting =
of the harbour.

The village fete is next Saturday. There will be the usual cake stall and dog=
 show, and I have somehow been talked into helping with the tombola.

We are looking forward to seeing you at the end of the month. The spare bed i=
s made up, and I have found the board game we used to play, so be ready for a=
 rematch.

The repairs to the roof are done at last. The builders were tidy and quick on=
ce they started, and we no longer need the bucket in the attic when it rains.

I bumped into your cousin at the station last week. She looked well and asked=
 to be remembered to you; she is still working at the library and seems to en=
joy it.

The tomatoes on the windowsill are finally ripening. It has taken all summer,=
 but the first few were delicious, and there are plenty more still turning re=
d.

We had a power cut on Tuesday evening and ended up playing cards by candlelig=
ht. It was rather fun, though the children were less keen once they realised =
the internet was off.

Give my love to everyone, and write when you can.

--=20
Aunt Rosa

